  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "The solution to the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x=3+y \\\\ 5y=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ncan be represented using matrix notation as\n\n| $x=$ {{field-1}} |\n| --- |\n\n| |  | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  | |  |  |  | |  | $3$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $10$ |  | | . |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} | $1$ |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $3$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $10$ |  |  |  |  |",
  "worked_solution": "First, we reorganize the equations so that they are in the right order, to get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}6x-y=3 \\\\ 0x+5y=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can represent this system of equations in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the solution, we need to calculate the inverse of the coefficient matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].$\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 5-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now represent the solution to this system as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
